window.CET4_BANKS.push({"id": "2024-06-3", "source": "2024年06月真题·第3套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: Suppose your university is seeking students’ opinions on whether university canteens should be open to the public. You are now to write an essay to express your view. You will have 30 minutes for the task. You will have 30 minutes for the task. You should write at least 120 words but no more than 180 words.", "sample": "Recently, our university has been collecting opinions on whether campus canteens should welcome\noutside visitors. In my view, opening them to the public is a wise move that benefits both the campus and\nthe community.\nFor one thing, university canteens usually offer clean and affordable meals, so opening them can relieve the\ndining pressure faced by nearby residents and office workers who struggle to find cheap lunches. For another,\nthe extra income earned from these visitors allows canteens to upgrade their equipment and even lower prices\nfor students. Admittedly, more diners may cause crowding and raise safety concerns; however, such problems\ncan be eased by setting fixed visiting hours and checking visitors at the gate.\nTherefore, I firmly believe that a properly managed open canteen is worthwhile. As long as student needs come\nfirst, sharing this resource makes our university a warmer part of society."}, "translation": {"prompt": "汉语中的 \"福\"字 (the character fu)表示幸福和好运，是中国传统文化中最常用的吉祥(auspicious) 符号之一。人们通常将一个大大的福字写在红纸上， 寓意期盼家庭幸福、社会安定、国家昌盛。春节贴福字是民间由来已久的习俗。为了欢庆春节， 家家户户都会将福字贴在门上或墙上， 表达对幸福生活的向往、对美好未来的期待。人们有时还将福字倒过来贴，表示幸福已到、好运已到。", "sample": "The Chinese character \"Fu\" means happiness and good fortune, and is one of the most commonly used auspicious symbols in traditional Chinese culture. People usually write a large character \"Fu\" on red paper, expressing their wish for family happiness, social stability and national prosperity. Pasting the character \"Fu\" during the Spring Festival is a long-standing folk custom. To celebrate the Spring Festival, every household pastes the character \"Fu\" on doors or walls, expressing their longing for a happy life and their expectation for a better future. Sometimes people also paste the character \"Fu\" upside down to indicate that happiness and good fortune have arrived."}, "listeningUrl": null, "clozePassage": "Over the coming decades, millions of jobs will be threatened by robotics and artificial intelligence. Despite intensive academic  __26__  on these developments, there has been little study on how workers  __27__  to being replaced through technology.\nTo find out, business researchers at TUM and Erasmus University Rotterdam conducted 11 studies and surveys with over 2,000 persons from several countries.\nThe findings show: In principle, most people view it more  __28__  when workers are replaced by other people than by robots or intelligent software. This preference  __29__  , however, when it refers to people's own jobs. When that is the case, the majority of workers find it less upsetting to see their own jobs go to robots than to other employees. In the long term, however, the same people see machines as more threatening to their future role in the workforce. These effects can also be observed among people who have recently become unemployed.\nThe researchers were able to identify the causes behind these  __30__  paradoxical results, too: People tend to  __31__  themselves less with machines than with other people. Consequently, being replaced by a robot or software  __32__  less of a threat to their feeling of self-worth. This reduced self-threat could even be observed when participants assumed that they were being replaced by other employees who relied on technological abilities such as artificial intelligence in their work.\n\"Even when unemployment results from the  __33__  of new technologies, people still judge it in a social context,\" says Christoph Fuchs, one of the authors of the study. \"It is important to understand these  __34__  effects when trying to manage the massive changes in the working world to minimize  __35__  in society.\"", "matchIntro": "No escape as 'snow day' becomes 'e-learning day'", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "Certain institutions, such as schools, are likely to close when bad weather, such as snow, flooding or extreme heat or cold, causes travel difficulties, power outages(断供), or otherwise endangers public safety. When snowy weather arrives in the US, it means the chance of school children benefiting from the long-standing tradition of the \"snow day\", when schools are forced to close and students get an unexpected day off."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The criterion for a snow day is primarily the inability of school buses to operate safely on their routes and danger to children who walk to school. Often, the school remains officially open even though buses do not run and classes are canceled. Severe weather that causes cancellation or delay is more likely in regions that are less able to handle the situation. Snow days are less common in more northern areas of the United States that are used to heavy winter snowfall, because municipalities are well equipped to clear roads and remove snow. In areas less accustomed to snow even small snowfalls of an inch or two may render roads unsafe."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Snow days are a familiar theme in American film and TV shows, with children getting the good news and then running outside for some seasonal snowman-building and snowball throwing, against a background of joyful pop music. But the tradition is now over for pupils in several US states such as South Carolina, Nevada, Georgia and Indiana. This academic year, many school boards have introduced policies which require students to work from home if the school is shut by snow or extreme weather. They are known as \"e-learning days\", which certainly sounds less fun than a snow day."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Teachers are also losing their snow days and instead will be expected to be on hand to take a virtual register and answer students' questions online. A pilot programme in a school district in Anderson County, South Carolina, has supplied students with electronic tablets loaded with assignments to complete in the event of a school closure. If it is successful, it could be rolled out across the state."}, {"label": "E", "text": "But some parents object to the new policy if the vigorous debate on the Facebook page of Anderson County school district is anything to go by. \"When it snows, let the kids enjoy it,\" said one commenter. Another said the decision would \"ruin school even more\", and someone else called snow days \"a fun part of childhood\". But supporters of the policy say it means children will miss fewer days of school. It will also bring to an end a less popular US high school tradition: the \"make-up day\", which requires students in many states to make up the time lost due to weather by working during school holidays."}, {"label": "F", "text": "Students in North Carolina already have several make-up days scheduled because of school closures during Hurricane Florence, which struck in September. Tom Wilson, the superintendent(主管) of Anderson County school district, said the change away from snow days makes practical and financial sense. He said technology has changed every profession, so it makes sense to use it to \"eliminate\" make-up days. Adam Baker of the Department of Education in Indiana said e-learning days were proving a \"great success\". He said most Indiana schools already use digital devices during lessons, so it was an \"easy decision\" to extend this to days when schools are closed. He denies the decision is depriving children of the chance to enjoy the snow. \"Students are still able to enjoy snow days and outside time,\" he said. \"Many have PE and science assignments that have them out enjoying the weather.\" But local school superintendents in Ohio are resisting proposals to adopt e-learning days. They fear that students without internet access at home will be disadvantaged by the policy, and superintendent Tom Roth is concerned that e-learning days will offer a lower quality of education."}, {"label": "G", "text": "There are also so-called \"blizzard bags\", with assignments that children take home ahead of an expected snow closure. But Mr Roth says it is not sufficient as a replacement. \"I think we still need the class time to give our kids the education that they deserve,\" he said. \"You can't get that with a blizzard bag or doing the work from home like that. It's not going to be as effective.\""}, {"label": "H", "text": "There is a long-running debate on whether missing days of school affects attainment. In England, there has been a focus on tackling absenteeism(旷课) from school. The Department for Education (DFE) published research in 2016 arguing that missing any days at school could have a negative impact on results. Even a few days lost in a year could be enough to miss out on getting a good exam grade, the DFE's research concluded. This differed from the findings of a study from Harvard University in the US, which concluded that missing a few occasional days because of the weather did not damage learning."}, {"label": "I", "text": "The Harvard study examined seven years of school results data and could not find any impact from snow closures. What caused more disruption was when schools tried to stay open in bad weather, even though many staff and pupils were absent. But weather can make a difference to school results, according to another piece of Harvard research published last summer. It's hot weather that has the negative impact. The results of 10 million school students were examined over 13 years and researchers found a \"significant\" link between years with extremely hot weather and lower results."}, {"label": "J", "text": "It's obvious that students should go to school every day to get the most out of education. In cases of extreme weather students don't always have that option. However, research shows that authorised absences from school such as during extreme weather are less problematic for students than absences that are not authorised. This is because unauthorised absences tend to reflect patterns and behaviours of student disengagement, or the possible negative attitudes of parents towards education that students adopt and carry with them through schooling. The level of impact on students' educational performance is all to do with the length of time that a student is absent from school and how regularly this occurs."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "It may sound surprising, but you don't have to be interested in fashion, or even in history, to enjoy Dress Codes: How the Laws of Fashion Made History. I happen to be interested in both, and ended up enjoying the book for completely different reasons.\nRichard Thompson Ford is a law professor, and you probably won't forget that for even one page. His carefully reasoned arguments, packed with examples, sound almost like reading a court opinion, only maybe wordier. You will probably never think of fashion as a trifle again.\nFord's thesis is that the best way to understand what particular fashions meant in any given era is to look at the restrictions placed on them. Through this lens, he shows us that the first laws passed in the 1200s to ensure that only the nobility were allowed to wear certain fabrics, colors and ornaments reflected the rise of the middle class, who were now able to imitate some of these fashions. The status of the upper classes was threatened; fashion was a tool to preserve it.\nFord takes the reader through the evolution of fashion while examining the underlying motivations of status, sex, power, and personality, which, he assumes, influenced all innovations in fashion in the past and which continue to influence us today. His writing is more than a little dense—dense with research, clauses, and precise adjectives and nouns. But there's also humor and enough interesting episodes to make the writing appealing. No one is spared his sharp analysis: not the easy targets of 19th century women's crippling(伤害身体的) fashions nor the modern uniforms of Silicon Valley T-shirts\nBut the greatest strength of this book (on fashion!) is its intellectual profoundness. Ford asks us to question unconscious beliefs, to realize that we almost never do so, to understand that the simplest choices are charged with meaning, and yet that meaning can and does change all the time. Consider the fact that a 1918 catalog insisted that boys and girls be dressed in the appropriate color. We believe our thinking today is evolved; Ford shows us it's not.", "47": "It may sound surprising, but you don't have to be interested in fashion, or even in history, to enjoy Dress Codes: How the Laws of Fashion Made History. I happen to be interested in both, and ended up enjoying the book for completely different reasons.\nRichard Thompson Ford is a law professor, and you probably won't forget that for even one page. His carefully reasoned arguments, packed with examples, sound almost like reading a court opinion, only maybe wordier. You will probably never think of fashion as a trifle again.\nFord's thesis is that the best way to understand what particular fashions meant in any given era is to look at the restrictions placed on them. Through this lens, he shows us that the first laws passed in the 1200s to ensure that only the nobility were allowed to wear certain fabrics, colors and ornaments reflected the rise of the middle class, who were now able to imitate some of these fashions. The status of the upper classes was threatened; fashion was a tool to preserve it.\nFord takes the reader through the evolution of fashion while examining the underlying motivations of status, sex, power, and personality, which, he assumes, influenced all innovations in fashion in the past and which continue to influence us today. His writing is more than a little dense—dense with research, clauses, and precise adjectives and nouns. But there's also humor and enough interesting episodes to make the writing appealing. No one is spared his sharp analysis: not the easy targets of 19th century women's crippling(伤害身体的) fashions nor the modern uniforms of Silicon Valley T-shirts\nBut the greatest strength of this book (on fashion!) is its intellectual profoundness. Ford asks us to question unconscious beliefs, to realize that we almost never do so, to understand that the simplest choices are charged with meaning, and yet that meaning can and does change all the time. Consider the fact that a 1918 catalog insisted that boys and girls be dressed in the appropriate color. We believe our thinking today is evolved; Ford shows us it's not.", "48": "It may sound surprising, but you don't have to be interested in fashion, or even in history, to enjoy Dress Codes: How the Laws of Fashion Made History. I happen to be interested in both, and ended up enjoying the book for completely different reasons.\nRichard Thompson Ford is a law professor, and you probably won't forget that for even one page. His carefully reasoned arguments, packed with examples, sound almost like reading a court opinion, only maybe wordier. You will probably never think of fashion as a trifle again.\nFord's thesis is that the best way to understand what particular fashions meant in any given era is to look at the restrictions placed on them. Through this lens, he shows us that the first laws passed in the 1200s to ensure that only the nobility were allowed to wear certain fabrics, colors and ornaments reflected the rise of the middle class, who were now able to imitate some of these fashions. The status of the upper classes was threatened; fashion was a tool to preserve it.\nFord takes the reader through the evolution of fashion while examining the underlying motivations of status, sex, power, and personality, which, he assumes, influenced all innovations in fashion in the past and which continue to influence us today. His writing is more than a little dense—dense with research, clauses, and precise adjectives and nouns. But there's also humor and enough interesting episodes to make the writing appealing. No one is spared his sharp analysis: not the easy targets of 19th century women's crippling(伤害身体的) fashions nor the modern uniforms of Silicon Valley T-shirts\nBut the greatest strength of this book (on fashion!) is its intellectual profoundness. Ford asks us to question unconscious beliefs, to realize that we almost never do so, to understand that the simplest choices are charged with meaning, and yet that meaning can and does change all the time. Consider the fact that a 1918 catalog insisted that boys and girls be dressed in the appropriate color. We believe our thinking today is evolved; Ford shows us it's not.", "49": "It may sound surprising, but you don't have to be interested in fashion, or even in history, to enjoy Dress Codes: How the Laws of Fashion Made History. I happen to be interested in both, and ended up enjoying the book for completely different reasons.\nRichard Thompson Ford is a law professor, and you probably won't forget that for even one page. His carefully reasoned arguments, packed with examples, sound almost like reading a court opinion, only maybe wordier. You will probably never think of fashion as a trifle again.\nFord's thesis is that the best way to understand what particular fashions meant in any given era is to look at the restrictions placed on them. Through this lens, he shows us that the first laws passed in the 1200s to ensure that only the nobility were allowed to wear certain fabrics, colors and ornaments reflected the rise of the middle class, who were now able to imitate some of these fashions. The status of the upper classes was threatened; fashion was a tool to preserve it.\nFord takes the reader through the evolution of fashion while examining the underlying motivations of status, sex, power, and personality, which, he assumes, influenced all innovations in fashion in the past and which continue to influence us today. His writing is more than a little dense—dense with research, clauses, and precise adjectives and nouns. But there's also humor and enough interesting episodes to make the writing appealing. No one is spared his sharp analysis: not the easy targets of 19th century women's crippling(伤害身体的) fashions nor the modern uniforms of Silicon Valley T-shirts\nBut the greatest strength of this book (on fashion!) is its intellectual profoundness. Ford asks us to question unconscious beliefs, to realize that we almost never do so, to understand that the simplest choices are charged with meaning, and yet that meaning can and does change all the time. Consider the fact that a 1918 catalog insisted that boys and girls be dressed in the appropriate color. We believe our thinking today is evolved; Ford shows us it's not.", "50": "It may sound surprising, but you don't have to be interested in fashion, or even in history, to enjoy Dress Codes: How the Laws of Fashion Made History. I happen to be interested in both, and ended up enjoying the book for completely different reasons.\nRichard Thompson Ford is a law professor, and you probably won't forget that for even one page. His carefully reasoned arguments, packed with examples, sound almost like reading a court opinion, only maybe wordier. You will probably never think of fashion as a trifle again.\nFord's thesis is that the best way to understand what particular fashions meant in any given era is to look at the restrictions placed on them. Through this lens, he shows us that the first laws passed in the 1200s to ensure that only the nobility were allowed to wear certain fabrics, colors and ornaments reflected the rise of the middle class, who were now able to imitate some of these fashions. The status of the upper classes was threatened; fashion was a tool to preserve it.\nFord takes the reader through the evolution of fashion while examining the underlying motivations of status, sex, power, and personality, which, he assumes, influenced all innovations in fashion in the past and which continue to influence us today. His writing is more than a little dense—dense with research, clauses, and precise adjectives and nouns. But there's also humor and enough interesting episodes to make the writing appealing. No one is spared his sharp analysis: not the easy targets of 19th century women's crippling(伤害身体的) fashions nor the modern uniforms of Silicon Valley T-shirts\nBut the greatest strength of this book (on fashion!) is its intellectual profoundness. Ford asks us to question unconscious beliefs, to realize that we almost never do so, to understand that the simplest choices are charged with meaning, and yet that meaning can and does change all the time. Consider the fact that a 1918 catalog insisted that boys and girls be dressed in the appropriate color. We believe our thinking today is evolved; Ford shows us it's not.", "51": "The art of persuasion means convincing others to agree with your point of view or to follow your course of action. For some of us, persuasion is an instinctive quality and the power of influencing comes naturally. For the rest of us, persuasion skills can be learned and developed over time.\nEmployers place a great value on employees with persuasion skills because they can impact several aspects of job performance. Besides, teamwork and leadership rely heavily on the power of persuasion to get things done. Without persuasion skills, employees may not be as committed to or convinced of the importance of an organization's vision and long-term mission. Effective use of persuasion skills will not only help get your coworkers excited about your ideas, it'll also help you motivate them to achieve a common goal.\nIn order to learn the art of persuasion at the workplace, you need to understand how to handle conflicts and reach agreements. Good communication is the first step in effective persuasion, but logic and reasoning are just as important. Before you can get somebody on-board with your goal, you should help them understand why they should pursue it. Using visual aids to back up your ideas can help communicate your ideas better and make compelling arguments so your listeners will come to a logical choice and become fully committed to your ideas and plans.\nSuccessful persuasion skills are based on your ability to have positive interactions and maintain meaningful relationships with people. In order to sustain those relationships, you must be able to work in their best interests as well. Your coworkers are more likely to agree with you when they succeed alongside you. The more they achieve and the greater progress they make, the more they trust your judgement and strength.\nWe persuade and get persuaded every day—we're either convincing or being convinced. A vast majority of people prefer collaboration and teamwork over traditional organizational structures; no one likes to be told what to do or to be pushed around. Therefore, organizations and leaders should adopt powerful persuasion skills to bring about necessary changes.", "52": "The art of persuasion means convincing others to agree with your point of view or to follow your course of action. For some of us, persuasion is an instinctive quality and the power of influencing comes naturally. For the rest of us, persuasion skills can be learned and developed over time.\nEmployers place a great value on employees with persuasion skills because they can impact several aspects of job performance. Besides, teamwork and leadership rely heavily on the power of persuasion to get things done. Without persuasion skills, employees may not be as committed to or convinced of the importance of an organization's vision and long-term mission. Effective use of persuasion skills will not only help get your coworkers excited about your ideas, it'll also help you motivate them to achieve a common goal.\nIn order to learn the art of persuasion at the workplace, you need to understand how to handle conflicts and reach agreements. Good communication is the first step in effective persuasion, but logic and reasoning are just as important. Before you can get somebody on-board with your goal, you should help them understand why they should pursue it. Using visual aids to back up your ideas can help communicate your ideas better and make compelling arguments so your listeners will come to a logical choice and become fully committed to your ideas and plans.\nSuccessful persuasion skills are based on your ability to have positive interactions and maintain meaningful relationships with people. In order to sustain those relationships, you must be able to work in their best interests as well. Your coworkers are more likely to agree with you when they succeed alongside you. The more they achieve and the greater progress they make, the more they trust your judgement and strength.\nWe persuade and get persuaded every day—we're either convincing or being convinced. A vast majority of people prefer collaboration and teamwork over traditional organizational structures; no one likes to be told what to do or to be pushed around. Therefore, organizations and leaders should adopt powerful persuasion skills to bring about necessary changes.", "53": "The art of persuasion means convincing others to agree with your point of view or to follow your course of action. For some of us, persuasion is an instinctive quality and the power of influencing comes naturally. For the rest of us, persuasion skills can be learned and developed over time.\nEmployers place a great value on employees with persuasion skills because they can impact several aspects of job performance. Besides, teamwork and leadership rely heavily on the power of persuasion to get things done. Without persuasion skills, employees may not be as committed to or convinced of the importance of an organization's vision and long-term mission. Effective use of persuasion skills will not only help get your coworkers excited about your ideas, it'll also help you motivate them to achieve a common goal.\nIn order to learn the art of persuasion at the workplace, you need to understand how to handle conflicts and reach agreements. Good communication is the first step in effective persuasion, but logic and reasoning are just as important. Before you can get somebody on-board with your goal, you should help them understand why they should pursue it. Using visual aids to back up your ideas can help communicate your ideas better and make compelling arguments so your listeners will come to a logical choice and become fully committed to your ideas and plans.\nSuccessful persuasion skills are based on your ability to have positive interactions and maintain meaningful relationships with people. In order to sustain those relationships, you must be able to work in their best interests as well. Your coworkers are more likely to agree with you when they succeed alongside you. The more they achieve and the greater progress they make, the more they trust your judgement and strength.\nWe persuade and get persuaded every day—we're either convincing or being convinced. A vast majority of people prefer collaboration and teamwork over traditional organizational structures; no one likes to be told what to do or to be pushed around. Therefore, organizations and leaders should adopt powerful persuasion skills to bring about necessary changes.", "54": "The art of persuasion means convincing others to agree with your point of view or to follow your course of action. For some of us, persuasion is an instinctive quality and the power of influencing comes naturally. For the rest of us, persuasion skills can be learned and developed over time.\nEmployers place a great value on employees with persuasion skills because they can impact several aspects of job performance. Besides, teamwork and leadership rely heavily on the power of persuasion to get things done. Without persuasion skills, employees may not be as committed to or convinced of the importance of an organization's vision and long-term mission. Effective use of persuasion skills will not only help get your coworkers excited about your ideas, it'll also help you motivate them to achieve a common goal.\nIn order to learn the art of persuasion at the workplace, you need to understand how to handle conflicts and reach agreements. Good communication is the first step in effective persuasion, but logic and reasoning are just as important. Before you can get somebody on-board with your goal, you should help them understand why they should pursue it. Using visual aids to back up your ideas can help communicate your ideas better and make compelling arguments so your listeners will come to a logical choice and become fully committed to your ideas and plans.\nSuccessful persuasion skills are based on your ability to have positive interactions and maintain meaningful relationships with people. In order to sustain those relationships, you must be able to work in their best interests as well. Your coworkers are more likely to agree with you when they succeed alongside you. The more they achieve and the greater progress they make, the more they trust your judgement and strength.\nWe persuade and get persuaded every day—we're either convincing or being convinced. A vast majority of people prefer collaboration and teamwork over traditional organizational structures; no one likes to be told what to do or to be pushed around. Therefore, organizations and leaders should adopt powerful persuasion skills to bring about necessary changes.", "55": "The art of persuasion means convincing others to agree with your point of view or to follow your course of action. For some of us, persuasion is an instinctive quality and the power of influencing comes naturally. For the rest of us, persuasion skills can be learned and developed over time.\nEmployers place a great value on employees with persuasion skills because they can impact several aspects of job performance. Besides, teamwork and leadership rely heavily on the power of persuasion to get things done. Without persuasion skills, employees may not be as committed to or convinced of the importance of an organization's vision and long-term mission. Effective use of persuasion skills will not only help get your coworkers excited about your ideas, it'll also help you motivate them to achieve a common goal.\nIn order to learn the art of persuasion at the workplace, you need to understand how to handle conflicts and reach agreements. Good communication is the first step in effective persuasion, but logic and reasoning are just as important. Before you can get somebody on-board with your goal, you should help them understand why they should pursue it. Using visual aids to back up your ideas can help communicate your ideas better and make compelling arguments so your listeners will come to a logical choice and become fully committed to your ideas and plans.\nSuccessful persuasion skills are based on your ability to have positive interactions and maintain meaningful relationships with people. In order to sustain those relationships, you must be able to work in their best interests as well. Your coworkers are more likely to agree with you when they succeed alongside you. The more they achieve and the greater progress they make, the more they trust your judgement and strength.\nWe persuade and get persuaded every day—we're either convincing or being convinced. A vast majority of people prefer collaboration and teamwork over traditional organizational structures; no one likes to be told what to do or to be pushed around. Therefore, organizations and leaders should adopt powerful persuasion skills to bring about necessary changes."}, "questions": [{"id": "2024-06-3-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) compare", "B) contradicts", "C) conventional", "D) debate", "E) disruptions", "F) drastically", "G) favorably", "H) guarantee", "I) introduction", "J) modifications", "K) poses", "L) psychological", "M) react", "N) reverses", "O) seemingly"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) debate\n名词\n【词性槽】形容词 intensive academic 后缺中心名词\n【依据】学术辩论与「研究 little study」对照\nintensive academic debate「激烈的学术辩论」与下半句 there has been little study\n「研究却少」形成 Despite 的让步对照——学界吵得热闹但实证研究稀少，转折逻辑\n顺畅。\n【竞争词】J) modifications 「修改」与 academic 搭配不通\nacademic modifications「学术修改」不是常规表达，也无法与下半句 little study 形成让步\n逻辑。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 J) —— modifications 形似「学术改动」。Despite intensive\nacademic __(26)__ on these developments 中考生若只看「学术」「发展」凭眼熟会选\nmodifications；防坑是认 Despite 的让步对照——下半句 little study 必须与空形成「热\n度 vs 实质」反差，只有 debate（学界激辩）能形成这种对照。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2024-06-3-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) compare", "B) contradicts", "C) conventional", "D) debate", "E) disruptions", "F) drastically", "G) favorably", "H) guarantee", "I) introduction", "J) modifications", "K) poses", "L) psychological", "M) react", "N) reverses", "O) seemingly"], "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M) react\n动词\n【词性槽】how workers ___ 主语后缺谓语\n【依据】react to 固定搭配「对……做出反应」\n空后紧跟介词 to (being replaced)，react to sth. 是「对某事做出反应」的固定搭配，与第\n二段「调研了 2,000 多人」对应——研究的就是工人对被替代的反应。\n【竞争词】A) compare compare 常用搭配是 with，不是 to being replaced\ncompare 后接 to 多用于比喻类比（compare A to B），不能讲「工人比较被替代」；语义上\n「研究工人怎样比较被替代」也讲不通。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2024-06-3-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) compare", "B) contradicts", "C) conventional", "D) debate", "E) disruptions", "F) drastically", "G) favorably", "H) guarantee", "I) introduction", "J) modifications", "K) poses", "L) psychological", "M) react", "N) reverses", "O) seemingly"], "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G) favorably\n副词\n【词性槽】view it more ___ 副词修饰动词\n【依据】view ... favorably 是固定搭配「赞许地看待」\n此句对比的是「被人取代」与「被机器取代」哪种更容易让人接受。view sth. favorably 意\n为「持赞许态度」，与后半句 than by robots 的比较结构契合：人们更赞许「被人取代」\n这种安排。\n【竞争词】F) drastically 「急剧地」语义偏激烈、与 view 不搭\ndrastically 多修饰变化（change drastically），view it drastically 不构成习惯搭配，也讲不通\n「更急剧地看待」。\nO) seemingly 「看似」无法被 more 修饰\nseemingly 表「表面上」，不带比较级语义，more seemingly 也不构成自然表达，且该词另有\n更贴合的空位归宿。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2024-06-3-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) compare", "B) contradicts", "C) conventional", "D) debate", "E) disruptions", "F) drastically", "G) favorably", "H) guarantee", "I) introduction", "J) modifications", "K) poses", "L) psychological", "M) react", "N) reverses", "O) seemingly"], "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N) reverses\n动词\n【词性槽】主语 This preference 后缺单数谓语\n【依据】偏好「翻转」契合段内反差\n前一句说「人们普遍更接受被人取代」，本句却用 however 引出「轮到自己时，大家反而\n觉得被机器取代不那么糟」，preference 完全反转，正是 reverses「逆转」的语义。\n【竞争词】B) contradicts contradict 需要带宾语\ncontradict 是及物动词「与……矛盾」，必须接宾语 contradict sth.，本句空后直接是逗号\nhowever，无宾语可接，结构不成立。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2024-06-3-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) compare", "B) contradicts", "C) conventional", "D) debate", "E) disruptions", "F) drastically", "G) favorably", "H) guarantee", "I) introduction", "J) modifications", "K) poses", "L) psychological", "M) react", "N) reverses", "O) seemingly"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) seemingly\n副词\n【词性槽】形容词 paradoxical 前修饰位\n【依据】「看似」与下文解释呼应\n本段开头说研究者找到了 these seemingly paradoxical results「这些看似矛盾结果」背后\n的原因——「看似」二字暗示后面会给出合理解释，与紧接其后的 People tend to\ncompare themselves less with machines 这条解释完美呼应。\n【竞争词】F) drastically 「极度地」修饰 paradoxical 程度过强\ndrastically paradoxical「极度矛盾」语义太满，与下文用心理机制平稳解释的论述基调不合；\n研究者既然能解释，矛盾就不是极端的。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 F) —— drastically 形似程度副词易选错。these __(30)__\nparadoxical results 中考生看到 paradoxical 容易想用「极度矛盾」加强语气，误选 F；防\n坑是看下一句——研究者紧接着就给出了原因解释，矛盾必是「看似」而非「极度」，锁\nO) seemingly。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2024-06-3-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) compare", "B) contradicts", "C) conventional", "D) debate", "E) disruptions", "F) drastically", "G) favorably", "H) guarantee", "I) introduction", "J) modifications", "K) poses", "L) psychological", "M) react", "N) reverses", "O) seemingly"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) compare\n动词\n【词性槽】tend to ___ themselves 不定式后接原形动词\n【依据】compare oneself with sb. 固定搭配\ncompare oneself with/to「把自己与……比较」是高频搭配；句意「人们倾向于把自己与机\n器比较得少」恰好解释下一句 poses less of a threat「威胁感更弱」的因果。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2024-06-3-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) compare", "B) contradicts", "C) conventional", "D) debate", "E) disruptions", "F) drastically", "G) favorably", "H) guarantee", "I) introduction", "J) modifications", "K) poses", "L) psychological", "M) react", "N) reverses", "O) seemingly"], "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K) poses\n动词\n【词性槽】动名词主语后缺谓语\n【依据】pose a threat 固定搭配「构成威胁」\n空后紧跟 less of a threat，pose a threat 是「构成威胁」的高频搭配，poses less of a\nthreat 即「构成的威胁较小」，与前文 People tend to compare themselves less with\nmachines 因果衔接：因不比较故威胁感更弱。\n【竞争词】B) contradicts 「反驳」与 threat 不搭配\ncontradict a threat 不构成搭配，且语义上「被替代反驳威胁」讲不通。\nN) reverses 「逆转」语义错位且要留给 29 题\nreverse a threat「逆转威胁」语义牵强；且 29 题 This preference ___ 明显需要 reverses「逆\n转」的呼应，应预留。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2024-06-3-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) compare", "B) contradicts", "C) conventional", "D) debate", "E) disruptions", "F) drastically", "G) favorably", "H) guarantee", "I) introduction", "J) modifications", "K) poses", "L) psychological", "M) react", "N) reverses", "O) seemingly"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) introduction\n名词\n【词性槽】the ___ of new technologies 介词框架定名词\n【依据】「新技术的引入」导致失业\nthe introduction of new technologies「新技术的引入/推出」是 unemployment results\nfrom 的合理原因——技术引入才会替代人力，逻辑链通顺。\n【竞争词】E) disruptions 搭配冲突且要留给 35 题\ndisruptions of new technologies 不是惯常表达（更常说 disruptions caused by\ntechnologies）；且 35 题 minimize ___ in society 明显是 disruptions 的归宿，应预留。\nJ) modifications 「修改」语义偏小、不致失业\nmodifications of new technologies 意为「对新技术的修改」，是技术本身被改，谈不上引发\n大规模失业，与因果不符。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2024-06-3-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) compare", "B) contradicts", "C) conventional", "D) debate", "E) disruptions", "F) drastically", "G) favorably", "H) guarantee", "I) introduction", "J) modifications", "K) poses", "L) psychological", "M) react", "N) reverses", "O) seemingly"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) psychological\n形容词\n【词性槽】名词 effects 前缺定语\n【依据】全段在讲心理机制\n第四段刚解释完人们「不愿与机器比较、被机器替代对自尊伤害更小」这类心理动因\nPeople tend to __(31)__ themselves less with machines than with other people.，第五\n段总结的 these effects 自然指上文这套心理反应，故用 psychological effects（心理效\n应）最贴。\n【竞争词】C) conventional 义偏「传统的」与上下文无关\nconventional effects 意为「常规效应」，文章讲的并不是某种「传统/常规」效应，而是研究新\n发现的心理反差，词义指向不符。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2024-06-3-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) compare", "B) contradicts", "C) conventional", "D) debate", "E) disruptions", "F) drastically", "G) favorably", "H) guarantee", "I) introduction", "J) modifications", "K) poses", "L) psychological", "M) react", "N) reverses", "O) seemingly"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) disruptions\n名词\n【词性槽】动词 minimize 缺宾语\n【依据】「社会动荡」正是要最小化的对象\nminimize disruptions in society 意为「把社会动荡降到最低」，与前文 manage\nthe massive changes（驾驭巨大变革）一脉相承——技术冲击必然带来社会动荡，需\n要 minimize。\n【竞争词】J) modifications 「修改」不是要消除的对象\nminimize modifications in society 意为「尽量少作修改」，不符合「应对巨变以减少负面冲\n击」的语义指向，修改本身不是负面事物。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2024-06-3-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "There is opposition to the practice of giving children assignments to take home before extreme weather forces a school closure.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】反对赛前带回家的作业\n【定位】There are also so-called \"blizzard bags\", with assignments that children take home ahead of\nan expected snow closure. But Mr Roth says it is not sufficient as a replacement.\n还有所谓的「blizzard bags」——是孩子在预计要因雪停课之前提前带回家的作业。但\nRoth 先生说，作为替代方案，它并不够。\n【改写】assignments to take home before extreme weather forces a school closure ↔\nassignments that children take home ahead of an expected snow closure\n逐词级同义替换\nThere is opposition to the practice ↔ But Mr Roth says it is not sufficient as a replacement\n态度词的具体化\n【辨邻】 F local school superintendents in Ohio are resisting proposals to adopt e-\nlearning days 是反对 e-learning day，不是反对 assignments to take home\nbefore extreme weather forces a school closure 这一 blizzard bag 做法，所以话\n题相邻、对象不同，排除。\nlocal school superintendents in Ohio are resisting proposals to adopt e-learning days", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-06-3-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "New policies adopted by many US schools require students to do online learning at home in case of a school closure.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】新政策要求停课时在家上网课\n【定位】This academic year, many school boards have introduced policies which require students to\nwork from home if the school is shut by snow or extreme weather. They are known as \"e-\nlearning days\", which certainly sounds less fun than a snow day.\n本学年，许多学校董事会都推出了新政策：如果学校因雪或极端天气关闭，学生须在家做功\n课。它们被称为「e-learning day」，听上去远不如「snow day」那么有趣。\n【改写】New policies adopted by many US schools ↔ many school boards have introduced policies\n施动方平移加同义替换\ndo online learning at home in case of a school closure ↔\nwork from home if the school is shut by snow or extreme weather\n条件状语同义改写\n【辨邻】 D A pilot programme in a school district in Anderson County, South Carolina 是单\n一学区的具体案例，与 many US schools 的「很多」在范围上不匹配，所以是下位干\n扰，不是定调源头。\nA pilot programme in a school district in Anderson County, South Carolina, has supplied students\nwith electronic tablets loaded with assignments to complete in the event of a school closure", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-06-3-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "According to some research, extreme hot weather negatively affects students' performance.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】极端高温拉低成绩\n【定位】It's hot weather that has the negative impact. The results of 10 million school students were\nexamined over 13 years and researchers found a \"significant\" link between years with\nextremely hot weather and lower results.\n真正带来负面影响的是高温天气。研究检视了 1000 万名学生 13 年的成绩，研究者发现极\n端高温年份与较低成绩之间存在「显著」关联。\n【改写】extreme hot weather ↔ extremely hot weather\n形容词副词同根替换\nnegatively affects students' performance ↔\nlink between years with extremely hot weather and lower results\n因果关系与结果指标的同义改写\n【辨邻】 H missing any days at school could have a negative impact on results 是「缺课-成\n绩」的负面关系，与 extreme hot weather 的「高温-成绩」主题不同，话题相邻但变\n量不同，排除。\nmissing any days at school could have a negative impact on results", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-06-3-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "There is a time-honoured tradition in the US for school kids to stay at home on \"snow days\".", "options": null, "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】悠久传统雪天在家\n【定位】When snowy weather arrives in the US, it means the chance of school children benefiting\nfrom the long-standing tradition of the \"snow day\", when schools are forced to close and\nstudents get an unexpected day off.\n当美国迎来下雪天，就意味着学童有机会享受历史悠久的「雪天假」传统——学校被迫关\n门，学生得到一天意外的休息。\n【改写】a time-honoured tradition ↔ the long-standing tradition\n形容词同义替换\nschool kids to stay at home on \"snow days\" ↔\nschools are forced to close and students get an unexpected day off\n结果性改写\n【辨邻】 C But the tradition is now over for pupils in several US states 强调 snow day 传统\n已结束，与 a time-honoured tradition 的「由来已久」定调相反，不是源头段。\nBut the tradition is now over for pupils in several US states such as South Carolina, Nevada,\nGeorgia and Indiana", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-06-3-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "Debates on social media show some parents are opposed to ending the \"snow day\" tradition.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】社交媒体上家长反对\n【定位】But some parents object to the new policy if the vigorous debate on the Facebook page of\nAnderson County school district is anything to go by. \"When it snows, let the kids enjoy it,\"\nsaid one commenter.\n但如果 Anderson County 学区 Facebook 主页上那场激烈辩论可作参考，一些家长是反对\n新政策的。一位评论者说：「下雪了，就让孩子去享受吧。」\n【改写】Debates on social media ↔\nthe vigorous debate on the Facebook page of Anderson County school district\n上下位概念同义\nsome parents are opposed to ending the \"snow day\" tradition ↔\nsome parents object to the new policy\n对象不同表述同义\n【辨邻】 F local school superintendents in Ohio are resisting proposals to adopt e-\nlearning days 是学监反对，不是题干所要求的家长反对，且 F 段也没有「社交媒体」\n这一渠道指纹，排除。\nlocal school superintendents in Ohio are resisting proposals to adopt e-learning days", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-06-3-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "In more northern regions of the US, school is less likely to be affected by snowy weather.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】更靠北的地区受雪影响更小\n【定位】Snow days are less common in more northern areas of the United States that are used\nto heavy winter snowfall, because municipalities are well equipped to clear roads and\nremove snow.\n在美国更靠北、习惯了大量冬雪的地区，雪天假并不那么常见，因为市政部门有充分的设备\n来清理道路、清除积雪。\n【改写】more northern regions of the US ↔ more northern areas of the United States\n近义名词替换\nschool is less likely to be affected by snowy weather ↔\nSnow days are less common in more northern areas of the United States\n因果与结果的等价转译", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-06-3-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "Research indicates absences from school with permission do not cause as many problems as those without permission.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】经许可缺课不如未经许可严重\n【定位】However, research shows that authorised absences from school such as during extreme\nweather are less problematic for students than absences that are not authorised.\n然而，研究显示：因极端天气等情况经许可的缺课，对学生造成的问题比未经许可的缺课\n更少。\n【改写】absences from school with permission ↔ authorised absences from school\n形容词与介短同义\ndo not cause as many problems as those without permission ↔\nare less problematic for students than absences that are not authorised\n比较句同义重构\n【辨邻】 H missing any days at school could have a negative impact on results 笼统讲缺\n课，未出现 authorised 与 not authorised 对照，与题干核心对照不匹配，排除。\nmissing any days at school could have a negative impact on results", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-06-3-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "There is objection to e-learning days owing to fear that students with no access to the Internet at home will suffer.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】反对 e-learning days 担心无网学生\n【定位】But local school superintendents in Ohio are resisting proposals to adopt e-learning days.\nThey fear that students without internet access at home will be disadvantaged by the policy,\nand superintendent Tom Roth is concerned that e-learning days will offer a lower quality\nof education.\n但 Ohio 的本地学监们正在抵制采用 e-learning days 的提议。他们担心家中没有网络的学\n生会因该政策处于劣势，学监 Tom Roth 还担心 e-learning days 会带来更低质量的教育。\n【改写】students with no access to the Internet at home ↔ students without internet access at home\n否定形式同义\nwill suffer ↔ will be disadvantaged by the policy\n结果词同义\nobjection to e-learning days ↔\nlocal school superintendents in Ohio are resisting proposals to adopt e-learning days\n态度词同义\n【辨邻】 E When it snows, let the kids enjoy it 与 a fun part of childhood 的反对理由是「剥\n夺孩子的雪天乐趣」，不是 students with no access to the Internet at home will\nsuffer 的「无网吃亏」，理由不符，排除。\nWhen it snows, let the kids enjoy it", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-06-3-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "In a pilot programme, students are given electronic devices to do assignments when schools are closed.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】试点项目发电子设备做作业\n【定位】A pilot programme in a school district in Anderson County, South Carolina, has supplied\nstudents with electronic tablets loaded with assignments to complete in the event of a\nschool closure.\nSouth Carolina 的 Anderson County 学区开展了一个试点项目，给学生发放装好作业的电\n子平板，以便在学校停课时完成作业。\n【改写】a pilot programme ↔ A pilot programme\n专有名词原样复现\nelectronic devices to do assignments when schools are closed ↔\nelectronic tablets loaded with assignments to complete in the event of a school closure\n上下位加条件状语改写\n【辨邻】 C many school boards have introduced policies which require students to work\nfrom home if the school is shut by snow or extreme weather 是政策的总定调，\n没有谈到 a pilot programme 与 electronic devices，与题干两条指纹都不符，\n排除。\nmany school boards have introduced policies which require students to work from home if the\nschool is shut by snow or extreme weather", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-06-3-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "Along-standing debate is going on over the impact of school absences on students' academic performance.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】缺课影响成绩的长期争论\n【定位】There is a long-running debate on whether missing days of school affects attainment.\n关于缺课是否影响学业成绩，长期以来一直存在争论。\n【改写】A long-standing debate ↔ There is a long-running debate\n形容词同义替换\nthe impact of school absences on students' academic performance ↔\nwhether missing days of school affects attainment\n名词化与动词句互转\n【辨邻】 I The Harvard study examined seven years of school results data 是具体研究案\n例，与 A long-standing debate 的总论腔不匹配；I 段并未陈述「长期争论」这一事\n实，只是给出研究结论，排除。\nThe Harvard study examined seven years of school results data and could not find any impact from\nsnow closures", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-06-3-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What does the author think of the book Dress Codes: How the Laws of Fashion Made History?", "options": ["A) It is read by people for entirely different reasons.", "B) It is meant for those interested in fashion history.", "C) It makes enjoyable as well as informative reading.", "D) It converts fashion into something for deliberation."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （主旨题）\n【判型】What does the author think of the book 问的是作者对整本书的看法，不针对任何细节\n段，属于全文评价层面的主旨态度问题。\n【拆句】主干 What does the author think of the book：think of 在此是「认为……怎么样」的固\n定搭配，of 后接评价对象；书名作 of 的宾语。无陷阱。\n【选项】A) 过度推断\nIt is read by people for entirely different reasons 把 I happen to be interested in\nboth, and ended up enjoying the book for completely different reasons 中「我个\n人因不同理由喜欢」错误推广到所有读者群体。\nB) 与原文相反\nIt is meant for those interested in fashion history 与 you don't have to be\ninterested in fashion, or even in history, to enjoy 直接矛盾——原文是「不必对这两\n者感兴趣也能享受」，选项却限定「专为感兴趣者而写」。\nC) ✓ 正确\nenjoy Dress Codes: How the Laws of Fashion Made History ↔ enjoyable\nintellectual profoundness ↔ informative\nenough interesting episodes to make the writing appealing ↔ enjoyable\nIt makes enjoyable as well as informative reading 用 enjoyable as well as\ninformative 两个形容词分别对应「P1/P4 阅读体验」和「P5 思想深度」两条主线，是\n唯一同时覆盖两面的选项。\nD) 过度推断\nIt converts fashion into something for deliberation 把 You will probably never\nthink of fashion as a trifle again 过度解读为「把时尚转化为可供深思的事物」，把作\n者对一句话的预测拔成全书定位。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-3-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "How can people best understand a particular fashion in an era, according to Ford?", "options": ["A) By examining the restraints imposed on it.", "B) By looking at what the nobility were wearing.", "C) By glancing at its fabrics, colors and ornaments.", "D) By doing a survey of the upper and middle classes"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】How can people best understand a particular fashion in an era, according to Ford?\n用 how 问具体方法，且明确归属 according to Ford，是定位 Ford 观点句的细节题。\n【拆句】主干 How can people understand…，best 修饰 understand 表程度；in an era 限定\nfashion 所处时代；句末 according to Ford 标注观点归属。无陷阱。\n【定位】Ford's thesis is that the best way to understand what particular fashions meant in any given\nera is to look at the restrictions placed on them.\nFord 的核心论点是：要理解某一时代里特定时尚的含义，最好的方式就是看强加在这些时\n尚之上的种种限制。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nlook at the restrictions placed on them ↔ examining the restraints imposed on it\n四个选项中只有 By examining the restraints imposed on it 完整还原「观察—限制」\n这一对核心语义，与预判完全等值。\nB) 拿例证替换方法\nBy looking at what the nobility were wearing 只截取 the nobility were allowed to\nwear certain fabrics, colors and ornaments 这处例证，没回到 Ford 真正提出的方法\n——「看限制」。\nC) 偷换概念\nBy glancing at its fabrics, colors and ornaments 取自 certain fabrics, colors and\nornaments，是 1200s 立法所限制的具体内容，被偷换成了「理解时尚的方法」。\nD) 无中生有\nBy doing a survey of the upper and middle classes 中 doing a survey 文中并无任\n何对应，Ford 谈的是「看限制」而非做问卷或调查。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-3-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What was the aim of the first laws passed regarding fashion in the 1200s?", "options": ["A) To facilitate the rise of the middle class.", "B) To loosen restrictions on dress codes.", "C) To help initiate some novel fashions", "D) To preserve the status of the nobles."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What was the aim of the first laws 问的是某一具体历史事件的目的，需要回原文找对应\n「目的/原因」表述。\n【拆句】主干 What was the aim of the first laws…？aim of 后接修饰首批法律的两个定语 passed\nregarding fashion 和 in the 1200s。无陷阱。\n【定位】the first laws passed in the 1200s to ensure that only the nobility were allowed to wear certain\nfabrics, colors and ornaments reflected the rise of the middle class, who were now able to\nimitate some of these fashions. The status of the upper classes was threatened; fashion was a\ntool to preserve it.\n1200 年代通过的最早一批法律，规定只有贵族才能穿戴某些面料、颜色与饰品；这些法律\n折射出中产阶级的崛起，因为他们已能模仿其中一些时尚。上层阶级的地位受到威胁，时尚\n成为维持这一地位的工具。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nTo facilitate the rise of the middle class 把「中产阶级崛起」当成目的，但原文是中\n产阶级崛起在前、立法在后，立法是为了反制这种崛起、保护贵族。\nB) 与原文相反\nTo loosen restrictions on dress codes 与原文「立法是限制」的方向完全相反，原文\n是 only the nobility were allowed to wear，明显是加紧而非放松。\nC) 无中生有\nTo help initiate some novel fashions 称立法是为了开启新潮流，文中只字未提；原文\n谈的是用法律压制模仿，而非鼓励创新。\nD) ✓ 正确\nThe status of the upper classes was threatened; fashion was a tool to preserve it ↔\nTo preserve the status of the nobles\nonly the nobility were allowed to wear certain fabrics, colors and ornaments ↔\nTo preserve the status of the nobles\n预判「维护贵族地位」直接对应 To preserve the status of the nobles，preserve、\nstatus、nobles 三个关键词全部命中，是直白的同义重述。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-3-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What does the author think of Ford's writing?", "options": ["A) It uses comparison and contrast in describing fashions of different eras.", "B) It makes heavy reading but is not lacking in humor or appeal.", "C) It is filled with interesting episodes to spare readers intolerable boredom.", "D) It is characteristic of academics in presenting arguments."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author think of Ford's writing? 评价对象是局部的 writing，不是全书，\n属于一段内的评价细节题。\n【拆句】主干 What does the author think of Ford's writing？think of 仍是固定搭配「对…怎么\n看」。无陷阱。\n【定位】His writing is more than a little dense—dense with research, clauses, and precise\nadjectives and nouns. But there's also humor and enough interesting episodes to make the\nwriting appealing.\n他的写作可以说相当密实——密集的研究、从句以及精准的形容词与名词。但其中也不乏幽\n默与足够多的有趣片段，使得整本书读来仍具吸引力。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nIt uses comparison and contrast in describing fashions of different eras 谈 Ford\n用了 comparison and contrast，P4 通篇没有这层评价。\nB) ✓ 正确\nHis writing is more than a little dense ↔ makes heavy reading\nthere's also humor and enough interesting episodes to make the writing appealing ↔\nnot lacking in humor or appeal\nIt makes heavy reading but is not lacking in humor or appeal 用 but 连接，分别\n对接原文 dense 与 But there's also humor and …appealing 两个分句，与预判完全\n一致。\nC) 过度推断\nIt is filled with interesting episodes to spare readers intolerable boredom 把原文\n「也有」夸成「充满」，把「使写作吸引人」放大为「拯救读者免于难以忍受的无\n聊」，与 dense 评价并列存在的事实矛盾。\nD) 以偏概全\nIt is characteristic of academics in presenting arguments 与 sound almost like\nreading a court opinion, only maybe wordier 有关，但题目问的是 P4 段的写作评\n价；且选项遗漏了「幽默与吸引力」这一面。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-3-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What does the author say is the greatest strength of Ford's book?", "options": ["A) Plentiful information.", "B) Meaningful choices.", "C) Evolved thinking.", "D) Intellectual depth"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author say is the greatest strength of Ford's book? 问「最大优点是什\n么」，文中必有显式表述，属细节定位题。\n【拆句】主干 What does the author say is the greatest strength…？嵌套宾语从句 the author\nsay + 主语 [the greatest strength of Ford's book] + 系动词 is，问的是表语的内容。无\n陷阱。\n【定位】But the greatest strength of this book (on fashion!) is its intellectual profoundness.\n但这本（讲时尚的！）书最大的优点，是它在思想上的深刻。\n【选项】A) 张冠李戴\nPlentiful information 把 dense with research 误当作本段「最大优点」，跨段错位，\n且 Ford 的写作密度在原文是写作风格而非最大优点。\nB) 以偏概全\nMeaningful choices 取自 the simplest choices are charged with meaning，那是支\n撑思想深度的细节，不是最大优点本身。\nC) 与原文相反\nEvolved thinking 直接对应 We believe our thinking today is evolved; Ford shows\nus it's not，作者明确指出今天的思维其实并未进化——把它当作 Ford 书的最大优点，\n与原文结论完全相反。\nD) ✓ 正确\nintellectual profoundness ↔ Intellectual depth\nIntellectual depth 与预判「思想上的深刻」语义一一对应，无任何缩放或偷换。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-3-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What does the author say about the ability to be persuasive in the first paragraph?", "options": ["A) People may either be born with it or be able to cultivate it.", "B) It proves crucial in making others follow one's course of action.", "C) It refers to the natural and instinctive power of influencing one's coworkers.", "D) People may view it as both a means to convince others and an art of communication."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author say about the ability to be persuasive in the first paragraph?\n是典型的 What does the author say + 段落限定，问的是作者在指定段落对某事的具体说\n法，无 infer/imply 字样。\n【拆句】题干 What does the author say about the ability to be persuasive in the first\nparagraph? 主干为 What does the author say about X，X 是 the ability to be\npersuasive；in the first paragraph 是地点状语修饰 say。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】For some of us, persuasion is an instinctive quality and the power of influencing comes\nnaturally. For the rest of us, persuasion skills can be learned and developed over time.\n对我们中的一些人来说，说服是与生俱来的天赋，影响他人的能力是天生的；对我们其余的\n人来说，说服技巧是可以随时间学习并培养的。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nFor some of us, persuasion is an instinctive quality ↔ born with it\npersuasion skills can be learned and developed over time ↔ be able to cultivate it\n预判要求“天生或后天习得”两端都覆盖，仅 People may either be born with it or be\nable to cultivate it 用 either...or 句式完整对应两端。\nB) 答非所问\nIt proves crucial in making others follow one's course of action 把首段对“能力来\n源”的说明换成了“能力的重要性”，回答的不是 What does the author say 这个角度的\n问题。\nC) 以偏概全\nIt refers to the natural and instinctive power of influencing one's coworkers 只截\n取了“天生本能”那一半（some of us），漏掉了“后天习得”那一半（the rest of us），\n且把场景窄化到 coworkers。\nD) 无中生有\nPeople may view it as both a means to convince others and an art of\ncommunication 中的 art of communication 在首段并未提及，原文只说 the art of\npersuasion，把 persuasion 偷换成 communication。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-3-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "Why are persuasion skills greatly valued in the workplace?", "options": ["A) They enable employees to be convinced of their long-term gains.", "B) They enable employees to trust their leaders unconditionally.", "C) They help motivate coworkers to strive for a common goal.", "D) They help an organization to broaden its vision effectively."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】Why are persuasion skills greatly valued in the workplace? 问的是被广泛重视的具体原\n因，是文中可直接读出的事实，不要求跨段综合推断。\n【拆句】题干 Why are persuasion skills greatly valued in the workplace? 是 Why + be + 过去\n分词 valued 构成的被动疑问句，主语是 persuasion skills；in the workplace 是地点状\n语。被动语态把动作发出方（雇主）省略了，考生需要意识到“被谁所重视”的隐含主语在原\n文是 employers。\n【定位】Effective use of persuasion skills will not only help get your coworkers excited about your\nideas, it'll also help you motivate them to achieve a common goal.\n有效运用说服技巧不仅有助于让你的同事对你的想法感到兴奋，也有助于你激励他们为实现\n共同目标而努力。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nThey enable employees to be convinced of their long-term gains 中的 long-term\ngains 在原文找不到对应，原文 long-term mission 谈的是组织的长期使命，并非员工\n的长远收益。\nB) 过度推断\nThey enable employees to trust their leaders unconditionally 中 unconditionally\n是绝对化表述，原文只谈“激励”，从未要求“无条件信任”，属于过度推断。\nC) ✓ 正确\nhelp you motivate them to achieve a common goal ↔\nhelp motivate coworkers to strive for a common goal\n预判“激励同事为共同目标”两要素，They help motivate coworkers to strive for a\ncommon goal 在句法结构上几乎是原文的平移，是最贴近预判的选项。\nD) 偷换概念\nThey help an organization to broaden its vision effectively 把原文的“让员工\ncommitted to vision”（员工对愿景的认同）偷换成“拓展组织的愿景”（broaden its\nvision），动作和受体都被换掉。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-3-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What should people do to learn the art of persuasion at the workplace?", "options": ["A) Acquire effective communication skills.", "B) Avoid getting involved in conflicts with others.", "C) Understand the reason for pursuing their goals.", "D) Commit themselves fully to their ideas and plans."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What should people do to learn the art of persuasion at the workplace? 提问“为达成\nX 该做什么”，答案是原文中显性陈述的前置条件，无需推断。\n【拆句】题干 What should people do to learn the art of persuasion at the workplace? 主干是\nWhat should people do，to learn... at the workplace 是不定式做目的状语。考生易把\nto learn 误读为定语修饰 do，正确理解应是“为了学会...，人们该做什么”。\n【定位】Good communication is the first step in effective persuasion, but logic and reasoning are just\nas important.\n良好的沟通是有效说服的第一步，但逻辑和推理同样重要。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nGood communication is the first step in effective persuasion ↔\nAcquire effective communication skills\n预判要求“先做好沟通”，Acquire effective communication skills 用 acquire 动作动词\n直译 first step 隐含的“获取动作”，匹配度最高。\nB) 偷换概念\nAvoid getting involved in conflicts with others 把原文 how to handle conflicts（处\n理冲突）偷换成 avoid... conflicts（回避冲突），原文从未让人避开冲突。\nC) 张冠李戴\nUnderstand the reason for pursuing their goals 把原文中的“帮别人理解为什么要追\n求该目标”张冠到“自己理解”，主语错位。\nD) 答非所问\nCommit themselves fully to their ideas and plans 描述的是听众被说服后的状态\n（fully committed），是“说服成功”的结果，而题干问的是说服者该做的前置条件。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-3-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "When are you more likely to succeed in persuading your coworkers?", "options": ["A) When they are convinced you work in their interests while sacrificing your own", "B) When they become aware of the potential strength of the judgements you make", "C) When they become aware of the meaningful relationships you keep with them.", "D) When they are convinced they will make achievements together with you."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】When are you more likely to succeed in persuading your coworkers? 问的是某客观条\n件下的可能性，文中第四段直陈条件句，可直接定位。\n【拆句】题干 When are you more likely to succeed in persuading your coworkers? 主干是\nWhen are you more likely to succeed，to succeed in doing 是固定搭配。考生易把\nmore likely 误读作纯感情色彩词，应理解为“在何种条件下，更可能”，相当于在问触发\n条件。\n【定位】Your coworkers are more likely to agree with you when they succeed alongside you.\n当你的同事与你一起取得成功时，他们更可能同意你的看法。\n【选项】A) 过度推断\nwhile sacrificing your own 是原文从未提及的“牺牲自我利益”，原文只说“为对方利益\n着想”，未要求牺牲自己。\nB) 偷换概念\nthe potential strength of the judgements you make 把原文 they trust your\njudgement and strength 中的并列两件事偷换成“判断的潜在力量”，concept 被悄悄\n合并改写。\nC) 答非所问\nmeaningful relationships you keep with them 是说服力的前提条件（说服者需要做\n的事），不是同事被说服的触发条件——题干问的是后者。\nD) ✓ 正确\nwhen they succeed alongside you ↔\nWhen they are convinced they will make achievements together with you\n预判“双方共同成功才更易被说服”，they will make achievements together with you\n是定位句 they succeed alongside you 的同义改写，方向一致。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-3-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "Why are organizations and leaders advised to adopt powerful persuasion skills to bring about necessary changes?", "options": ["A) To convince employees of the value of collaboration", "B) To allow for the preferences of most people of today", "C) To improve on traditional organizational structures", "D) To adapt to employees' ever-changing working styles"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】Why are organizations and leaders advised to adopt powerful persuasion skills to\nbring about necessary changes? 问的是该建议的事实依据，文中末段直陈，无须推断。\n【拆句】题干 Why are organizations and leaders advised to adopt powerful persuasion skills\nto bring about necessary changes? 是 Why + 被动 are advised to do 的疑问句，to\nbring about... 是不定式做目的状语。该结构隐含的动作发出方是“作者”，被建议方是\norganizations and leaders；考生需识别这是在问作者给出此建议的依据。\n【定位】A vast majority of people prefer collaboration and teamwork over traditional organizational\nstructures; no one likes to be told what to do or to be pushed around. Therefore, organizations\nand leaders should adopt powerful persuasion skills to bring about necessary changes.\n绝大多数人更喜欢协作与团队合作，而非传统组织结构；没人喜欢被指使或被任意摆布。因\n此，组织和领导者应当采用有力的说服技巧来带来必要的变革。\n【选项】A) 答非所问\nTo convince employees of the value of collaboration 把“顺应大多数人偏好”换成了\n“说服员工相信协作的价值”——原文是员工自己已经偏好协作，无需再被说服其价值。\nB) ✓ 正确\nA vast majority of people prefer collaboration and teamwork over traditional organizational\nstructures\n↔ To allow for the preferences of most people of today\n题干问“为什么被建议这么做”，To allow for the preferences of most people of\ntoday 用 To do 不定式直接给出目的-原因，是最贴合预判的答案。\nC) 无中生有\nTo improve on traditional organizational structures 把“改善传统组织结构”列为目\n的，但原文 traditional organizational structures 出现的语境是“人们更偏好协作而非\n传统组织结构”，并未把“改善”它作为说服技巧的目的。\nD) 无中生有\nTo adapt to employees' ever-changing working styles 中的 ever-changing\nworking styles（不断变化的工作方式）原文未提，原文谈的是“偏好”而非“工作方式的\n变化”。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}], "listeningSharedWith": "2024-06-2"});
